<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congo–Ocean Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chemin de fer Congo-Océan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congo–Ocean Railway runs for about 500 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pointe-noire">Pointe-Noire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>. It was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a> between 1921 and 1934 with forced labour recruited across the federation, including Ubangi-Shari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>. Disease, hunger and brutal conditions killed many thousands of workers, and the scandal was exposed by writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-gide">André Gide</a> and Albert Londres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congo-ocean-railway.jpg" alt="Congo–Ocean Railway" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jkan997 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Railways_in_Congo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The line runs 502 kilometres from the Atlantic port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pointe-noire">Pointe-Noire</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> and bypasses the rapids of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>, so that from Brazzaville river boats can go up the Congo and its tributaries, including the Oubangui River to Bangui. It is built to a gauge of 1,067 mm and includes the 1,690-metre Bamba tunnel and 14 large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinforced-concrete">reinforced concrete</a> viaducts, with gradients of up to 1 in 50. The first locomotives were 2-8-2 tender engines and articulated tank engines with six driving axles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1921 the French colonial administration contracted the Société de Construction des Batignolles to build the railway with forced labour recruited from what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>. Workers suffered rampant physical abuse, poor housing and hygiene and extreme deprivation, and deaths from industrial accidents and diseases such as malaria are estimated at more than 17,000 by the time construction ended in 1934. Resentment of conscripted labour and other oppression led to the Kongo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wara">Wara</a> rebellion of 1928 to 1931. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> did not ratify the International Labour Organization's Forced Labour Convention of 1930 until 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1962 a branch was built to Mbinda near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> to meet the COMILOG Cableway and carry manganese ore to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pointe-noire">Pointe-Noire</a>; the cableway closed in 1986, when Gabon built its own railway, but the branch stayed in use. The line closed for six years from the start of the civil war in 1997 and reopened in 2004. On 21 June 2010 a derailment between Bilinga and Tchitondi threw four carriages into a ravine and killed at least 60 people. A passenger train, La Gazelle, with Korean-manufactured passenger cars, was introduced in 2012, and ten diesel locomotives from Electro-Motive Diesel entered service in 2015. The railway is a state-owned enterprise.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congo–Ocean Railway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Concession companies, compulsory cotton growing, timber extraction and forced labour, notably for the <b>Congo-Ocean railway</b>, provoked revolts such as the Kongo-Wara rising in Ubangi-Shari.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Congo-Ocean railway</b> from Brazzaville to Pointe-Noire, built between 1921 and 1934, cost the lives of many thousands of conscripted workers.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-congo">Middle Congo</a></li><li><blockquote>Concession companies forced people to collect rubber and later grow cotton, and thousands were conscripted to build the <b>Congo-Ocean railway</b> in Middle Congo.</blockquote><a href="/state/ubangi-shari">Ubangi-Shari</a></li></ul></section>
